ames>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Efficacy of olanzapine and olanzapine-fluoxetine combination in the treatment of bipolar I depression</article-title><source>Arch Gen Psychiatry</source><volume>60</volume><fpage>1079</fpage><lpage>88</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14609883</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b83-ndt-3-855"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tollefson</surname><given-names>GD</given-names></name><name><surname>Sanger</surname><given-names>TM</given-names></name><name><surname>Lu</surname><given-names>YL</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1998</year><article-title>Depressive signs and symptoms in schizophrenia &#x02013; A prospective blinded trial of olanzapine and haloperidol</article-title><source>Arch Gen Psychiatry</source><volume>55</volume><fpage>250</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9510219</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b84-ndt-3-855"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vavrusova</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Quetiapine in the treatment of non-psychotic depression (P.2 E 037)</article-title><source>Int J Neuropsychopharmacol</source><volume>5</volume><issue>Suppl 1</issue></citation></ref><ref id="b85-ndt-3-855"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vieta</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Reinares</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Corbella</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Olanzapine as long-term adjunctive therapy in treatment-resistant bipolar disorder</article-title><source>J Clin Psychopharmacol</source><volume>21</volume><fpage>469</fpage><lpage>73</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11593070</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b86-ndt-3-855"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Viner</surname><given-names>MW</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>YX</given-names></name><name><surname>Bakshi</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Low-dose risperidone augmentation of antidepressants in nonpsychotic depressive disorders with suicidal ideation</article-title><source>J Clin Psychopharmacol</source><volume>23</volume><fpage>104</fpage><lpage>6</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12544386</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b87-ndt-3-855"><citation citation-type="book"><collab>World Health Organization</collab><source>World Health Report 2001 Mental Health: New Understanding</source><publisher-loc>New Hope Geneva, Switzerland</publisher-loc><publisher-name>World Health Organization</publisher-name><year>2001</year></citation></ref><ref id="b88-ndt-3-855"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yargic</surname><given-names>LI</given-names></name><name><surname>Corapcioglu</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Kocabasoglu</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>A prospective randomized single-blind, multicenter trial comparing the efficacy and safety of paroxetine with and without quetiapine therapy in depression associated with anxiety</article-title><source>Int J Psychiatry Clin Pract</source><volume>8</volume><fpage>205</fpage><lpage>11</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b89-ndt-3-855"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yatham</surname><given-names>LN</given-names></name><name><surname>Goldstein</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Vieta</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Atypical antipsychotics in bipolar depression: Potential mechanisms of action</article-title><source>J Clin Psychiatry</source><volume>66</volume><fpage>40</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16038601</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b90-ndt-3-855"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zarate</surname><given-names>CA</given-names></name><name><surname>Rothschild</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Fletcher</surname><given-names>KE</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>Clinical predictors of acute response with quetiapine in psychotic mood disorders</article-title><source>J Clin Psychiatry</source><volume>61</volume><fpage>185</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10817103</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><table-wrap id="t1-ndt-3-855" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Published studies of augmentation of an antidepressant with quetiapine in patients with unipolar depression</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Study</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Design</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">N</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Antidepressant/mood stabilizer</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Quetiapine</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Primary efficacy measure and result</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Depression remission and response rates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b84-ndt-3-855">Vavrusova 2002</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4-week open-label</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">citalopram 40 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">200 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MADRS, HAM-D, HAM-A, CGI, and PANSS; Significant improvement in depression, guilt, suicidal ideation, anxiety, sleep, and retardation</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b14-ndt-3-855">Doree et al 2007</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8-week, open-label</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">antidepressant plus lithium (n = 10)<break/>antidepressant plus quetiapine (n = 10)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">up to 800 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in HAM-D and MADRS from baseline to endpoint; Mean HAM-D improved from 25.8 down to 4.4 and mean MADRS improved from 35.7 to 6.7</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">80% response rate on HAM-D and MADRS for quetiapine;<break/>80% remission rate on HAM-D and MADRS for quetiapine</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b2-ndt-3-855">Adson et al 2004</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9-week open-label, Subjects had comorbid anxiety disorder</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MDD (n = 6); GAD (n = 5)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">fluoxetine (n = 3)<break/>paroxetine (n = 4)<break/>sertraline (n = 1)<break/>citalopram (n = 3)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">mean dose 180 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HAM-D, HAM-A, SAI baseline to endpoint;<break/>HAM-D significantly decreased from a mean of 20.27 to 5.64<break/>HAM-A significantly reduced from 24.45 to mean of 5.82</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10/11 (91%) responders</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b88-ndt-3-855">Yargic et al 2004</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8-week, prospective, single-blind</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">112</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Paroxetine alone (n = 54)<break/>Paroxetine plus quetiapine (n = 58)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">up to200 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in HAM-D from baseline to endpoint;<break/>Decrease in HAM-D scores was significantly greater in combined therapy group than with paroxetine alone (p &#x0003c; 0.008)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b30-ndt-3-855">Hussain et al 2005</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3-year, randomized, double blind</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">72</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">paroxetine monotherapy<break/>venlafaxine<break/>monotherapy<break/>paroxetine plus quetiapine<break/>venlafaxine plus quetiapine</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">not reported</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in HAM-D from baseline to endpoint;<break/>Significant improvement in depressive symptoms and the development of remission occurred more frequently in the paroxetine plus quetiapine group</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b61-ndt-3-855">Pathak et al 2005</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">case series in adolescents</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">fluoxetine (n = 3)<break/>venlafaxine (n = 1)<break/>sertraline (n = 4)<break/>citalopm (n = 1)<break/>escitalopram (n = 1)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">mean dose 275 &#x000b1; 190.4 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CGI improvement from baseline</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7/10 (70%) responders</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b78-ndt-3-855">Targum et al 2005</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4-week, open label</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">22</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">sertraline (n = 4)<break/>fluoxetine (n = 3)<break/>citalopram (n = 7)<break/>paroxetine (n = 3)<break/>venlafaxine (n = 5)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">mean dose 105.9 &#x000b1; 65.6 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in HAM-A and HAM-D from baseline to endpoint; Mean HAM-A significantly improved from 25.6 to 9.2( p &#x0003c; 0.001) and mean HAM-D improved from 15 to 7.2 (p &#x0003c; 0.001) at endpoint</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b13-ndt-3-855">Devarajan et al 2006</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">naturalistic case series</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">sertraline (n = 6)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">200&#x02013;300 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HAM-D-21 baseline to endpoint;<break/>Addition of quetiapine improved ratings at week 5&#x02013;6</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6/6 (100%) responders<break/>5/6 (83%) remitters</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b66-ndt-3-855">Sagud et al 2006</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20-week, open-label</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">18</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">reboxetine + paroxetine (n = 2)<break/>reboxetine + sertraline (n = 1)<break/>paroxetine + maprotiline (n = 4)<break/>paroxetine (n = 2)<break/>fluvoxamine (n = 2)<break/>sertraline (n = 1)<break/>moclobemide (n = 1)<break/>carbamazepine (n = 3)<break/>lamotrigine (n = 2)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">mean dose 315 &#x000b1; 109 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in HAM-D from baseline; By wk 2, HAM-D insomnia subscale significantly reduced; by wk 4, HAM-D total score and anxiety subscale score significantly improved; by wk 5, HAM-D depressed mood subscale significantly reduced</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14/18 (78%) responders<break/>11/18 (61%) remitted</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b5-ndt-3-855">Baune et al 2007</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4-week, open-label</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MDD (n = 21);<break/>BPD (n = 6)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">venlafaxine (n = 26); escitalopram (n = 1)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">300&#x02013;800 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in HAM-D from baseline to endpoint;<break/>Mean HAM-D significantly improved from 20.2 to 9.4 (p &#x0003c; 0.001) at endpoint</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn1-ndt-3-855"><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> CGI, Clinical Global Improvement; GAD, generalized anxiety disorder; HAM-A, Hamilton Rating Scale for Anxiety; HAM-D, Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression; MADRS, Montgomery Asberg Depression Rating Scale; MDD, major depressive disorder; BPD, bipolar depressive disorder; PANSS, Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale; SAI, State Anxiety Inventory.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="t2-ndt-3-855" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Unpublished randomized studies of augmentation of an antidepressant with quetiapine in patients with unipolar depression</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Study</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Design</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">N</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Antidepressant/mood stabilizer</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Quetiapine</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Primary efficacy measure and result</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Depression remission and response rates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b41-ndt-3-855">Khullar et al 2006</xref><xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn3-ndt-3-855">*</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8-week, randomized, placebo-controlled, double blind</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">16</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SSRI/SNRI plus quetiapine<break/>SSRI/SNRI plus placebo</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Average dose 350 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Change in HAM-D, HAM-A and MADRS from baseline to endpoint;<break/>Significantly greater mean change in HAM-D scores (&#x02212;11.9 vs &#x02212;4.9; p &#x0003c; 0.05) and MADRS scores(&#x02212;14.9 vs &#x02212;5.3; p &#x0003c; 0.05), in quetiapine vs placebo group</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Not reported</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b45-ndt-3-855">Mattingly et al 2006</xref><xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn3-ndt-3-855">*</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8-week, randomized, placebo-controlled, double blind</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">36</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SSRI/SNRI plus quetiapine<break/>SSRI/SNRI plus placebo</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mean dose 268 &#x000b1; 71.1 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in HAM-D and MADRS from baseline to endpoint;<break/>Significant improvement in HAM-D scores (&#x02212;16.7 vs &#x02212;9.8; p &#x0003c; 0.01) and MADRS scores(&#x02212;17 vs &#x02212;8.7; p &#x0003c; 0.05), in quetiapine vs placebo group</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HAM-D response 67% vs 27%<break/>HAM-D remission 43% vs 15%</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b46-ndt-3-855">McIntyre et al 2006</xref><xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn3-ndt-3-855">*</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8-week, randomized, placebo-controlled, double blind</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SSRI/SNRI plus quetiapine<break/>SSRI/SNRI plus placebo</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mean dose 202 &#x000b1; 93 mg/day</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in HAM-D and HAM-A from baseline to endpoint;<break/>Significant improvement in HAM-D scores (&#x02212;11.2 vs &#x02212;5.5; p &#x0003c; 0.01) and HAM-A scores(&#x02212;12.5 vs &#x02212;5.9; p &#x0003c; 0.01), in quetiapine vs placebo group</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HAM-D response 48% vs 28%<break/>HAM-D remission 31% vs 17%<break/>HAM-A response 62% vs 28%<break/>HAM-A remission 41% vs 17%</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn2-ndt-3-855"><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> HAM-A, Hamilton Rating Scale for Anxiety; HAM-D, Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression; MADRS, Montgomery Asberg Rating Scale for Depression; SNRI, selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor; SSRI, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor.</p></fn><fn id="tfn3-ndt-3-855"><label>*</label><p>Investigator-initiated study with funding support obtained from Astra Zeneca.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>